```html
<!-- 1️⃣  Embed the map (static image) -->
<img
    src="https://maps.googleapis.com/maps/api/staticmap?
        center=34.052235,-118.243683
        &zoom=15
        &size=600x400
        &maptype=roadmap
        &markers=label:S%7C34.052235,-118.243683   <!-- State Library marker -->
        &path=fillcolor:0x0000ff33|color:0x0000ff|weight:2|
            34.052235,-118.243683|
            34.053690,-118.2423732|
            34.05381,-118.242751
        &key=YOUR_API_KEY"
    alt="Map showing the State Library and three circular areas"
    width="600"
    height="400"
/>

<!-- 2️⃣  (Optional) If you prefer an interactive map instead of a static image,
      you can use the Google Maps JavaScript API.  The code below creates the same
      view, adds the marker, and draws the circles.  Replace YOUR_API_KEY with
      a valid key that has Maps JavaScript API enabled. -->
<script src="https://maps.googleapis.com/maps/api/js?key=YOUR_API_KEY"></script>
<div id="interactiveMap" style="width:600px;height:400px;"></div>
<script>
  function initMap() {
    const center = { lat: 34.052235, lng: -118.243683 };
    const map = new google.maps.Map(document.getElementById('interactiveMap'), {
      zoom: 15,
      center: center,
      mapTypeId: 'roadmap'
    });

    // State Library marker
    new google.maps.Marker({
      position: center,
      map: map,
      title: 'State Library'
    });

    // Helper to create a circle (radius in metres)
    const makeCircle = (lat, lng) => new google.maps.Circle({
      strokeColor: '#0000FF',
      strokeOpacity: 0.8,
      strokeWeight: 2,
      fillColor: '#0000FF',
      fillOpacity: 0.3,
      map: map,
      center: { lat, lng },
      radius: 50   // 50 m – adjust as needed
    });

    // Three circles
    makeCircle(34.052235, -118.243683);
    makeCircle(34.053690, -118.2423732);
    makeCircle(34.05381,  -118.242751);
  }

  // Initialise the map once the page loads
  window.addEventListener('load', initMap);
</script>
```

### How to use it

1. **Static image version** – just copy the `<img …>` tag into your page.  
   *Replace `YOUR_API_KEY` with a valid Google Maps Static API key.*  
   The `markers` parameter adds the “State Library” pin, and the `path` parameter draws a semi‑transparent blue polygon that visually represents the three circular points (the static API does not support true circles, so a polygon is used as a close visual approximation).

2. **Interactive version** – copy the `<script>` block, the `<div id="interactiveMap">`, and the JavaScript that follows.  
   This version uses the **Google Maps JavaScript API**, which can render true circles and lets users pan/zoom.

Both snippets are self‑contained; just ensure the API key you use has the appropriate Google Maps services enabled.

